# `a[i] + b[i]`: beside a text `undefined` is written out, so a read may give NaN for a hole only where the other operand is known to be a number or `undefined`.
# Of the read that stands first the other is not built yet. What Link says of that later read is held by a stop only on some of the roads it may be built by (not under a key that is a text, not where the type of the container answers),
# so under `+` the first read keeps `undefined`; the later one finds the first built, `number | undefined`, and gives NaN. Under `-`, `*`, `/` NaN and `undefined` answer alike beside any value: there both give NaN.
# f1 = sums, f2 = named
